<!-- ─────────────── §01 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-mismatch" data-code="mismatch">
<div class="section-no">01 — The OS Mismatch</div>
<h2>App-Centric OSes Were Never Designed for Agents</h2>
<p>Every modern mobile operating system — Android, iOS — is built around a single assumption: <em>a human is driving</em>. Interfaces are designed for touch. Apps live in their own silos. Permission models track app boundaries, not data flows. One app runs in the foreground at a time.</p>
<p>AI agents violate every one of these assumptions. An agent shopping for shoes needs to compare prices across JD, Taobao, and Amazon — but the OS forces it to launch each app separately, navigate pixel-by-pixel through GUIs designed for fingers, and re-read the full screen context at every step. Sensitive data — payment cards, home addresses — flows freely through the agent's context window. Transient toast notifications vanish before the agent can poll them.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">paradigm_shift.svg — Stock Android vs. Agent-Native AOHP</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">The same shopping task, two paradigms</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:280px">
    <canvas id="mismatch-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-top:.875rem">
      <button class="step-nav" id="mis-toggle" style="padding:.3rem 1rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">Toggle View</button>
      <span id="mis-label" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);line-height:2rem">Showing: Stock Android</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>AOHP (Android Open Harness Project) proposes a different contract: <strong>treat the AI agent as a first-class OS actor</strong>, on equal footing with apps and the OS itself. Instead of forcing agents to adapt to human-facing interfaces, the OS adapts its interfaces to agent workflows. The result is a fork of AOSP that preserves Android's entire app ecosystem while layering three agent-oriented primitives on top.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">The Three AOHP Primitives</div>
  <span class="math-var">P1</span> = Personalized Service Composition  <span class="math-comment">→ cross-app task entrances</span>
  <span class="math-var">P2</span> = Efficient Agent Interfaces          <span class="math-comment">→ structured UI, parallel exec, events</span>
  <span class="math-var">P3</span> = Secure Information Flow              <span class="math-comment">→ vault tokens, taint tracking</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §02 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-entrances" data-code="entrances">
<div class="section-no">02 — Generated Service Entrances</div>
<h2>The OS Composes Capabilities Across App Silos</h2>
<p>The most visible change in AOHP is what it does to the user interface layer: it makes UIs <em>generated rather than predefined</em>. A conventional app exposes whatever its developer decided to show. AOHP lets the OS agent synthesize a fresh <strong>service entrance</strong> tailored to the user's current task.</p>
<p>Consider the shopping task again. A generated shopping entrance aggregates product search from JD, Taobao, and Amazon; normalizes product attributes into a unified schema; applies the user's stored preferences (budget, size, preferred delivery window); and exposes a single comparison interface. The user interacts with the concept of "shopping" — the OS agent handles service discovery, invocation, memory management, and policy enforcement under the hood.</p>

