<p>AOHP maintains three memory tiers to make personalization safe. <strong>Persistent profile memory</strong> stores stable preferences (shoe size, budget ranges). <strong>Task-local memory</strong> stores transient state (candidate products, partially-completed forms). <strong>Sensitive memory</strong> stores private values through sandbox indices — the actual plaintext never leaves a trusted component. Personalization can be shared, audited, and revoked without depending on any individual app's data model.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §03 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-interfaces" data-code="interfaces">
<div class="section-no">03 — Efficient Agent Interfaces</div>
<h2>Four Access Modes — Always Take the Cheapest Path</h2>
<p>Stock Android gives agents one mode of interaction: navigate the GUI. Even when a task could be accomplished with a single API call, the agent must scroll, tap, and screenshot its way through a human-facing interface. AOHP provides four access modes, ordered by agent-friendliness:</p>

<div class="mode-grid">
  <div class="mode-card api">
    <div class="mode-card-title">① API</div>
    <div class="mode-card-cost">~20 tokens / step</div>
    <div class="mode-card-desc">Direct structured call to an app or system service. Lowest overhead, highest accuracy. AOHP exposes app capabilities as agent-callable APIs where available.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="mode-card cli">
    <div class="mode-card-title">② CLI / Shell</div>
    <div class="mode-card-cost">~30 tokens / step</div>
    <div class="mode-card-desc">Native sandbox runtime — OS-managed compute substrate for data transformation, file processing, and long-running services without touching app GUIs.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="mode-card sUI">
    <div class="mode-card-title">③ Structured UI</div>
    <div class="mode-card-cost">~100 tokens / step</div>
    <div class="mode-card-desc">Semantic abstraction of the GUI — removes rendering noise, exposes widget semantics. Richer than raw screenshots; ~5× cheaper than full visual parsing.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="mode-card gui">
    <div class="mode-card-title">④ Rendered GUI</div>
    <div class="mode-card-cost">~500 tokens / step</div>
    <div class="mode-card-desc">Raw screenshot fallback for apps with custom rendering or anti-automation logic. AOHP makes this mode explicit so agents can avoid it when alternatives exist.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Beyond the interface modes, AOHP introduces three efficiency primitives that stock Android lacks entirely:</p>

<h3>Parallel Background Execution</h3>
<p>Mobile OSes couple app execution to the physical display. An agent waiting for a slow JD product search can't simultaneously run the Taobao query — the foreground serializes everything. AOHP uses <strong>lightweight virtual displays</strong> to decouple execution from the screen, letting agents run multiple waiting-heavy workflows in parallel without preempting the active session.</p>

<h3>Unified File Shortcut</h3>
<p>Cross-app workflows constantly pass files between steps — download an attachment, reformat it, upload it to another service. On stock Android these handoffs are implicit: the agent must infer what happened from screenshots and per-app pickers. AOHP treats files as <strong>first-class task objects</strong>, reflecting GUI file interactions as structured observations and letting agents invoke system share flows programmatically.</p>

<h3>Event Stream Abstraction</h3>
<p>Transient events — toast notifications, bank OTPs, timer alerts — often vanish before an agent can poll them. AOHP's <strong>notification buffer</strong> intercepts and retains these short-lived messages, and its <strong>sensor streaming</strong> interface lets agents subscribe to hardware sensor data without repeated polling.</p>

