<!-- ─────────────── §04 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-security" data-code="security">
<div class="section-no">04 — Secure Information Flow</div>
<h2>Sensitive Data Never Enters the Agent Context</h2>
<p>Every tool-using agent faces the same vulnerability: <em>sensitive data flows through the agent's context window</em>. The agent needs to perform a payment — so it reads the payment card number, processes it, and passes it to the API call. At every step that plaintext is exposed to the LLM, logged in tool call history, and potentially exfiltrated by an indirect prompt injection attack in the web page the agent is browsing.</p>

<p>AOHP flips this model. Sensitive data is <strong>sanitized at the source</strong>, before it ever reaches the agent context. Here is what the agent actually sees for a payment task:</p>

<div class="vault-demo">
  <div class="vault-row">
    <div class="vault-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.15)">🏦</div>
    <span class="vault-label">App UI shows</span>
    <span class="vault-value plain">4242-4242-4242-4242</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">→ sanitized →</span>
    <span class="vault-value token">&lt;payment-card:a3f9&gt;</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">← agent sees this</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vault-row">
    <div class="vault-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.15)">🏠</div>
    <span class="vault-label">Address field</span>
    <span class="vault-value plain">北京市朝阳区XX路88号</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">→ sanitized →</span>
    <span class="vault-value token">&lt;home-address:7b2c&gt;</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">← agent sees this</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vault-row">
    <div class="vault-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.15)">📱</div>
    <span class="vault-label">Phone number</span>
    <span class="vault-value plain">138-0013-8000</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">→ sanitized →</span>
    <span class="vault-value token">&lt;phone:c1d8&gt;</span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">← agent sees this</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vault-row" style="margin-top:.75rem;padding-top:.75rem;border-top:1px solid var(--border)">
    <div class="vault-icon" style="background:rgba(16,185,129,.1)">✓</div>
    <span class="vault-label">Payment action</span>
    <span style="font-size:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)">Agent submits: <span style="color:var(--purple-l)">vault_exec(&lt;payment-card:a3f9&gt;, amount=¥320)</span></span>
    <span class="vault-arrow">→</span>
    <span class="vault-value ok">Trusted executor mediates</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The <strong>data vault</strong> stores real values behind opaque typed identifiers. When the agent needs to <em>use</em> sensitive data — fill in a form field, submit a payment, share a file — it submits an intent to the <strong>trusted vault executor</strong> using the placeholder. The executor checks policy, obtains user approval when required, and performs the operation inside a trusted environment. If the result is still sensitive, the executor returns another placeholder rather than plaintext.</p>

<h3>Taint Tracking Across App Boundaries</h3>
<p>Sanitization protects entry points; <strong>data-flow taint tracking</strong> preserves provenance after sensitive data is used. Once sensitive data enters AOHP, it carries taint metadata through copying, transformation, composition, and transfer. At every policy-relevant boundary — external APIs, file writes, GUI submissions — AOHP checks tainted data before allowing it to proceed. The taint path also provides an auditable trace: which source reached which sink through which task steps.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">taint_flow.canvas — Animated taint propagation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Watch sensitive data flow through the system</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="taint-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
